Let's Understand the Entire Packet Journey:
-------------------------------------------

    1) Imagine PC0 sends: ping 192.168.20.20
    2) PC0 checks is 192.168.20.20 inside my subnet: No
    3) PC0 says I will send to 192.168.10.1
    4) Before sending, PC0 needs the router's MAC address
    5) It sends an ARP Request: Who has 192.168.10.1
    6) The router replies: I am 192.168.10.1, My MAC is AA:AA:AA:AA
    7) PC0 stores this is ARP table
    8) PC0 creates an Ethernet Frame
        Source MAC PC0
        Destination MAC Router
        Source IP 192.168.10.10
        Destination IP 192.168.20.20
    9) Notice:
        The MAC destination is the router, but the IP destination is still PC1
        The destination between Layer 2 (MAC) and Layer 4 (IP) is fundamental
    10) The switch receives the frame
        It knows it came from VLAN 10
        before sending it over the trunk, it adds: 802.1Q Tag
        VLAN ID = 10
    11) The router receives the tag frame:
        it reads: VLAN 10
        it sends to G0.0/10
        The router examines the destination IP: 192.168.20.20
        It decides: This belongs to 192.168.20.0/24
        G0/0.20

        - The router changes the Layer 2 header (new source and destination MAC address)
        - because it's forwarding to a new network segment, but the IP source and destination remain the same (except for fields like TTL that are updated)
    12) The router sends the frame back to the switch with VLAN 20 tagging
    13) The switch removes the tag and forwards the same only to fa0/2, where PC1 is connected
    14) PC1 receives the ping and sends the the replay back through the same process in reverse
























